Parallel on-the-fly Crystal algorithm
=====================================

Implementation of the Crystal algorithm for global exploration of molecular configuration space:
a driver that walks a lattice of reaction coordinates under an energy ceiling, a pool of parallel
workers that evaluate lattice points with an external quantum-chemistry program, and the
refinement and clustering stages applied to the accepted points.

Contents
--------

  reaction_discovery_2025/   Reaction discovery in DASA photoswitches and bilirubin.
                             Pandey and Liang, J. Chem. Theory Comput. 2025, 21, 4704.
                             doi:10.1021/acs.jctc.5c00324

  ci_seam_2024/              Conical-intersection seam search for stilbene and 1,3-butadiene.
                             Pandey, Poirier and Liang, J. Chem. Theory Comput. 2024, 20, 4778.
                             doi:10.1021/acs.jctc.4c00292

  crystal.f90                Original 2022 single-file implementation, kept for reference,
  crystalNov16.f90           together with its submission script.
  submit.sh

The remainder of this file documents reaction_discovery_2025; ci_seam_2024 has its own README.
QUICKSTART gives a minimal two-worker example.

Requirements
------------

  Fortran         Intel Fortran (ifort). gfortran 10 or newer also works, with
                  -ffree-line-length-none -fallow-argument-mismatch
  Quantum chem.   MOPAC-PI (FOMO-CASCI) with geomeTRIC for bilirubin;
                  ChemShell 3.7.1 with ORCA and xTB for aqueous DASA;
                  TeraChem for gas-phase DASA
  Scheduler       SLURM
  Python          3.8 or newer, with numpy; MDTraj for the RMSD clustering pass

fix_paths.sh rewrites the conda environment name and the MOPAC-PI path in the run scripts:

  CONDA_ENV=myenv MOPACPI=/path/to/mopacpi.x ./fix_paths.sh

Layout
------

  reaction_discovery_2025/
    bilirubin_pm6fom_FINAL/         bilirubin, PM6-FOMO-CASCI with geomeTRIC and MOPAC-PI
    bilirubin_minimize_stage/       minimization stage for the same system
    dasa_yowy_re5/                  DASA aqueous QM/MM, lattice exploration
    dasa_aqueous_yowy_re4_FINAL/    DASA aqueous QM/MM, refinement: minimization,
                                    clustering and the NEB transition-state searches
    dasa_yow6/                      DASA in gas phase and implicit solvent, TeraChem GFN2-xTB

The two aqueous DASA directories are consecutive stages of one campaign: dasa_yowy_re5 runs the
exploration and holds the driver, the 1.pun seed and the worker-cloning scripts;
dasa_aqueous_yowy_re4_FINAL accumulates the accepted points and refines them, and therefore
carries no driver of its own.

Pipeline
--------

1. Build.

     ifort p.f90 -o a.out                                            # driver
     ( cd oldSecondary      && ifort s2.f90 -o b.out )               # accept or reject
     ( cd oldSecondary/test && ifort s3.f90 -o c.out                 # per-substep gate
                            && ifort convert.f90 -o convert.out )    # QM input generation

2. Seed. 1.xyz (bilirubin, 79 atoms) or 1.pun (DASA, 1212-atom solvated snapshot). The seed
   energy is the reference for the Ecut0 ceiling and must match the value set in p.f90.

3. Generate the workers and start the run.

     ./regenerate_workers.sh 400   # bilirubin: clone oldSecondary into secondary1..400
     ./loadFoundlings.sh           # DASA: clone oldSecondary, count taken from xt
     sbatch saout.sh               # driver: creates the buckets, seeds yesfolder, runs a.out
     ./batch30.sh                  # submit the workers

   The driver walks the lattice and hands points to the workers through flat files: measur.txt
   is the claim token, shelll.txt the pending shell, coord.txt the claimed point, status.txt the
   per-worker state. Each worker claims a point and builds the target geometry, writes the
   constraint values for each relaxed-scan interval with maketera.py (TeraChem or MOPAC) or
   makechm.py (ChemShell), relaxes it, gates each substep against emax, and takes the maximum
   energy over the substeps as the barrier for the accept-or-reject decision. convert.f90
   regenerates the quantum-chemistry input at each step and is where the excited-state root and
   the MOPAC OCCUP group are written.

   Output is sorted into yesfolder (accepted), nofolder, newlat, climbinglat, dissociated,
   failed and allfolder.

4. Minimization, unconstrained, from every accepted point.

     sbatch min/makeSubmit.sh      # one directory per accepted point, staged from yesfolder
     sbatch min/submissions.sh     # submit them

   For DASA the equivalent is minimize/<N>/minimize.chm with minimize.sh: a ChemShell hybrid
   optimization without constraints, tolerance 0.00045.

5. Extraction and clustering.

     ficOutput.py, purify.py, dissociated.py, bad_list.py, statistics.py
         restore the canonical atom order, discard fragmented geometries, tabulate results

     bilirubin cluster/: unique_1-list.py, unique_2-sort.py, unique_3-few.py reduce the
         geometries to a unique set; h-rmsd.py performs the heavy-atom RMSD pass with MDTraj

     DASA cluster/cluster.py: classifies structures by the four triene dihedrals (|d| < 90
         taken as cis) together with bond connectivity at a 1.6 A cutoff

6. Refinement (DASA). One directory per pair of minima (1_1067, 29-51, 32-41, 60-41, 70-41s,
   1366_1433, 41-41ht_dft), each with a TeraChem QM/MM nudged-elastic-band input ttc.in at
   wB97X-D3/6-31G* with the outer solvent frozen, plus replace_coord.py, parmconv.py and
   align_interpolated.py. geodesic/ holds the interpolated initial guesses, and
   41_gas_open/tc.in is the gas-phase reoptimization after the water is removed.

Worker generation
-----------------

Workers are clones of oldSecondary. In the bilirubin campaign each worker also carries an index
in its claim routine, so that worker i ignores lattice points below index i rather than all
workers contending for the same early points:

  secondary<i>/s1.py    if current == 0 or current < <i>:     for i = 1 .. bbb-1
                        if current == 0:                      for i = bbb

regenerate_workers.sh performs the clone and applies that edit; loadFoundlings.sh, which is the
original script, performs the clone only. The DASA workers are plain clones and need no index,
and each campaign's loadFoundlings.sh already carries the correct worker count, which must agree
with bbb in p.f90 and with the loop range in batch30.sh.

Run inputs
----------

Seed geometries 1.xyz, 1.pun and geom.xyz; MOPAC-PI templates gradient.dat; AMBER topology
dasa-carved_ID.prmtop and .pdb; DL_POLY CONFIG, FIELD and CONTROL; quantum and active region
definitions qm_tb.txt and active_tb.txt; the explicit degree-of-freedom list dl.in; per-interval
constraints constraints_1.txt to constraints_3.txt; the ChemShell driver move.chm; and the
neighbour move vectors radiux.txt.

Notes
-----

  - gradient.dat must end with an OCCUP group whose number of values equals the active-space
    orbital count; for OPEN(4,4) that is "2.0 2.0 0.0 0.0". A mismatch aborts in MOPAC-PI with a
    state.F90 end-of-file error.

  - MOPAC keyword lines must not exceed 80 columns and are continued with "+". Longer lines are
    silently truncated.

  - The published bilirubin results used geomeTRIC with the dihedral-constraint tolerance in
    internal.py relaxed from 1e-6 to 1e-4.

  - The worker watchdogs copy the input geometry into allfolder when they terminate a stalled
    job, so allslurms should be cross-checked against allfolder when reanalyzing a run.

Repository contents
-------------------

Directories that the scripts generate in large numbers - the worker directories and the
per-point minimization directories - are represented here by a single instance under its
original name, and are regenerated by regenerate_workers.sh, loadFoundlings.sh and
min/makeSubmit.sh. Results, scratch files and compiled binaries are not included.
COLLECTION_STATS.txt lists the selection in full.

